  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "ghaIG9hZk5C+ZTBG0NaKnl5idPM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:18.095Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The queue organises jobs in a Map keyed by `(documentId, scope, branch)`, but the guarantee it gives is stronger than that key suggests: **execution is serialized per document even across scopes and branches.** Jobs for different documents proceed in parallel; two jobs touching the same document never overlap, whichever scopes they name.\n\nThat is why the architecture lists ordering, not throughput, as the first reason for async job processing \u2014 and it is what makes a decision that reads one scope to authorize a write in another safe on the local reactor, without any additional lock.\n\nA job carries its own retry state:\n\n```ts\ntype Job = {\n  id: string;\n  kind: \"mutation\" | \"load\";\n  documentId: string; scope: string; branch: string;\n  actions: Action[];        // for mutations\n  operations: Operation[];  // for loads (sync)\n  queueHint: string[];      // job dependencies\n  maxRetries: number;\n  errorHistory: ErrorInfo[];\n};\n```\n\n`kind` splits the two entry points: a **mutation** carries actions to reduce, a **load** carries operations that already exist elsewhere and arrived by sync. `queueHint` is how one job waits for another, and `errorHistory` accumulates across retries rather than being overwritten, so a job that failed three different ways can be diagnosed after the fact.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 Core Concepts \u203a Job; Components \u203a Queue; Design Decisions \u203a Why Async Job Processing\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:18:18.095Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:18.095Z"
    }
  },
